{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Because you did this.</b> Rashi explained: from here we learn that one does not argue in merit of a seducer (מסית), etc. And if you ask: if so, how would a seducer ever be put to death — he would plead, 'such are the words of the master and such are the words of the disciple' — whose words do you heed? Rather, one may answer: one cannot exempt a human seducer with this, since he is warned not to seduce; but the serpent was not warned not to seduce, and if the Holy One, blessed be He, had asked him, 'why did you do this?' he would have exempted himself by saying, 'such are the words of the master,' etc. Rather, since he did not plead this, we do not plead it for him. And if you ask: but is it not explicitly stated that there is no seducer except for idolatry? Rather, from the fact that he said to them 'and you shall be like Elohim,' from this we learn that this is idolatry." "<b>Cursed are you.</b> The Holy One, blessed be He, began to curse them in the order in which they performed the transgression: the serpent, who began first, was cursed first, and after him Chava, and after her Adam." "<b>From all cattle and from all beast of the field.</b> Rashi explained: if from cattle he was cursed, from beast of the field all the more so, for we find that the suffering of gestation of a large cattle animal is greater than the suffering of gestation of a small wild animal. Our Sages established the days of gestation of the serpent as seven years. And this is what it says: cursed are you more than the cattle, in the manner and manner that it is cursed more than the beast of the field. Meaning: just as the cattle that drags out its days of gestation — this is the donkey — was cursed more than the beast of the field that shortens its days of gestation — this is the cat — after the days of gestation of the cat, seven weeks, and of the donkey, close to fifty weeks, so he was cursed more than that cattle animal: after these, they come to seven years. And the vav of <b>and from all</b> is superfluous. Another interpretation: <b>From all cattle and from all beast of the field</b> — there is no cattle or beast that goes on its belly, but you — <b>on your belly you shall go.</b> And to all cattle and to all beast of the field I have given every plant for food, but you — <b>dust you shall eat all the days of your life</b>: since your mouth is in the ground, the dust enters your mouth and you eat it against your will." "<b>And dust you shall eat.</b> This means virgin soil. And this is what it says: all the days of your life you shall toil to seek after virgin soil for your food, and this is the penalty imposed upon him." "<b>On your belly you shall go.</b> From here onward."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:02:49.962913999Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}